23:38:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You raise a fair point: a typical tall building elevator descent takes about a minute, which might seem slow compared to the rapid pressure changes in a chamber study. Intuitively, you might think the ear has plenty of time to equalize. But let's examine the actual numbers. ### 1. The rate of pressure change in a fast elevator A modern skyscraper elevator can descend at about **10 meters per second (33 ft/s)**. That's a vertical speed of **2,000 feet per minute**. Pressure changes with altitude at roughly **0.036 mb per foot** near sea level. So the rate of pressure increase during descent is: \[ 2,000 \text{ ft/min} \times 0.036 \text{ mb/ft} = 72 \text{ mb/min} \] That is **72 millibars per minute**. ### 2. How fast does the eustachian tube equalize? The eustachian tube opens passively only about **once every 1–2 minutes** during normal swallowing (which happens about once per minute when awake). Each opening allows only a small amount of air to pass — typically enough to equalize a pressure difference of about **5–10 mb**. At 72 mb/min, the pressure in your middle ear is increasing by more than 1 mb every second. If you swallow only once per minute, you would allow equalization of perhaps 5–10 mb, but during that minute the pressure has built up by 72 mb. That leaves a growing deficit of about **62 mb** at the end of the minute — far above the threshold for sensation (~10–20 mb). Even if you swallow more often (e.g., every 10–15 seconds), the total equalization capacity per swallow is limited. The net effect is that the pressure differential can easily exceed the perception threshold within 10–20 seconds. ### 3. How does this compare to a pressure chamber? Many chamber studies use rates of **50–100 mb/min**, exactly in the range of a fast elevator. A typical chamber protocol might descend at 100 mb/min over 2 minutes. That is very close to the elevator's 72 mb/min. So the chamber studies are **not** using unrealistically rapid changes — they are using rates that closely match real elevator descents. The fact that subjects in those chambers consistently report stuffiness is directly applicable. ### 4. Why a one-minute descent is enough - **Time to threshold:** At 72 mb/min, the pressure differential reaches the 10–20 mb perception threshold in about **8–15 seconds**. So even within the first few seconds of descent, you can start feeling stuffy. - **Accumulation:** If you don't equalize actively (e.g., by yawning or Valsalva), the differential continues to grow, reaching 72 mb in one minute. That is a very noticeable pressure. ### 5. Your personal counterexample (short ride) is still separate Your experience of stuffiness in a five-floor ride (pressure change ~1.5 mb, rate ~15 mb/min) is far below both the threshold and the rate that overwhelms the tube. That confirms that your stuffiness has a different cause. But for a tall building descent at 72 mb/min, the physics clearly predicts a pressure imbalance that most people will feel. ### Conclusion A one-minute elevator descent at 2,000 ft/min produces a pressure change rate of ~72 mb/min — which is **faster than the ear's passive equalization ability** and matches the rates used in chamber studies. So the chamber studies are directly relevant. Your intuition that "a minute is plenty of time" is correct only if the eustachian tube could equalize continuously, but it cannot. The rate of change is the key factor, and in tall building elevators it is high enough to cause noticeable stuffiness in most people.
