00:10:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Great question! You'd think saltwater fish would constantly lose water to their salty environment—and you'd be right. But they've evolved clever solutions to avoid dehydration. **The core problem:** Seawater is saltier than a fish's internal fluids. Through osmosis, water naturally tries to leave the fish’s body to dilute the surrounding salt. Without adaptations, they'd shrivel up. **The solutions vary by group:** 1. **Bony fish (e.g., tuna, clownfish):** - They **drink seawater constantly**. This might sound counterintuitive, but it's step one. - They **pump out excess salt** through specialized cells in their gills (chloride cells), actively excreting sodium and chloride ions. - Their kidneys produce very small amounts of concentrated urine (with some salts and lots of waste), conserving water. 2. **Cartilaginous fish (e.g., sharks, rays):** - They take a different approach. Instead of fighting the salt gradient, they **raise their internal saltiness** by retaining **urea** and **trimethylamine oxide (TMAO)** in their blood and tissues. - This makes their internal fluids about as salty as (or slightly saltier than) seawater, so water doesn't rush out—it may even trickle in. - Their gills also excrete excess salt, and they have a special gland (rectal gland) that removes extra salt from their blood. 3. **Marine mammals (e.g., whales, seals):** - They don't drink seawater at all—they get almost all their water from the food they eat (fish, squid, etc.), which has a much lower salt content. - Their kidneys are incredibly efficient, producing urine that is **even saltier than seawater**, allowing them to flush out excess salt without losing too much water. So in short: bony fish drink and pump out salt; sharks and rays store urea to match the environment; and marine mammals avoid drinking seawater altogether by relying on their prey. Each is a perfect adaptation to life in a saline world!
