00:16:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve put your finger on the crucial distinction: **a technical standard is not the same as an effective plan.** Yes, the IETF wrote IPv6. Yes, it works. But if by “plan” you mean a coordinated, enforced, and timely strategy to move the entire internet off IPv4, then the honest answer is: **no, one was never actually put in place.** ### Why the word “plan” misleads here In engineering, a plan typically includes: - A clear goal and timeline. - Incentives or mandates to drive adoption. - Consequences for non‑compliance. - Support for phasing out the old system. IPv6 had none of these: - **No deadline.** Registries stopped giving out new IPv4 blocks, but existing holders could keep their addresses forever. There was no “sunset” date for IPv4. - **No mandate.** No government or global body required ISPs or content providers to deploy IPv6. The US federal government mandated it for its own agencies in 2005, then quietly postponed compliance. - **No market penalty for delay.** In fact, the opposite: delaying IPv6 deployment saved money (no new gear, no training) as long as NAT and address trading worked. - **No kill switch.** You can’t just turn off IPv4 overnight — billions of legacy devices (printers, industrial controllers, smart TVs from 2014) would break. So IPv4 limps on, supported by a thriving secondary market for address blocks. ### The result: a non‑plan that worked by accident What actually happened is that the industry *improvised*: - NAT/CGNAT emerged as a cheap, universal patch. - A grey market for IPv4 addresses (transfers) gave price signals — but only after exhaustion. - IPv6 was deployed piecemeal where it solved a real pain (e.g., mobile carriers with millions of subscribers per IPv4 address). This is the opposite of a plan: it’s **reactive, fragmented, and mostly driven by short‑term cost avoidance**. ### So was there ever a real plan? **In theory, yes — RFCs exist.** **In practice, no — no one ever forced the transition.** Your skepticism is entirely justified. The internet’s governance model (voluntary standards, no central authority, profit‑driven stakeholders) made a coordinated global migration nearly impossible. The “plan” was essentially: *we’ll write the specs, and hope the market figures it out.* The market figured out a way to stall indefinitely. ### What this means going forward - IPv4 will not die. It will be traded, NATed, and tunneled for decades. - IPv6 adoption will continue to grow slowly, but likely never reach 100% of devices or networks. - We will live in a permanent dual‑stack world with translation gateways, which is messy but functional. So to answer your question directly: **No, an effective plan was never put in place. We got a technical blueprint, a decade of inaction, and a patchwork of workarounds that became the de facto solution.** The internet survived — but not because of good planning.
