00:20:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The idea of a "centre of the universe" is a natural one, but it turns out that the universe doesn't have a centre—at least not in the way we normally think of one. Here’s why: 1. **The universe is homogeneous and isotropic** – On the largest scales, the universe looks the same in every direction (isotropic) and has roughly the same density everywhere (homogeneous). This means there is no special location that stands out as the "centre". 2. **The Big Bang wasn’t an explosion from a single point** – A common misconception is that the Big Bang was like a bomb going off in a pre-existing empty space, with matter flying outward from a central point. In reality, the Big Bang was the expansion of space itself. Everywhere in the universe was once packed together, and the expansion happened uniformly at all points. So the "beginning" happened everywhere at once. 3. **Expansion has no centre** – Imagine a balloon with dots drawn on its surface. As you inflate the balloon, each dot sees all other dots moving away. No dot is at the centre; the centre would be inside the balloon, not on its two-dimensional surface. Similarly, our three-dimensional universe expands in a way that every observer sees galaxies receding from them. There is no preferred point from which everything originates. 4. **Observational evidence** – Maps of the cosmic microwave background (the afterglow of the Big Bang) show that the universe is incredibly uniform. If there were a centre, we would expect to see some pattern or direction pointing to it, but none is observed. In short, we can’t find the centre of the universe because there is no centre to find. Every point in the universe is equally valid as a reference location.
